{"name":"Owlet","hex":"#90845f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"owlet","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Diminutive of owl.","A young owl; owling."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/owlet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Strigidae","blocks":[{"text":"The true owls or typical owls are one of the two generally accepted families of owls, the other being the barn owls and bay owls (Tytonidae). This large family comprises roughly 230 living or recently extinct species in 23 genera. The Strigidae owls have a cosmopolitan distribution and are found on every continent except Antarctica."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Eastern_Screech_Owl.jpg/330px-Eastern_Screech_Owl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strigidae","wikidata":"Q26012"},{"title":"Noctuidae","blocks":[{"text":"The Noctuidae, commonly known as noctuid moths or owlet moths, and their caterpillars as cutworms or armyworms, are a family of moths. Taxonomically, they are considered the most difficult family in the superfamily Noctuoidea because the classification of many of the clades is constantly changing with new research, along with that of other families of the Noctuoidea. It was considered the largest family in Lepidoptera for a long time, but after regrouping Lymantriinae, Catocalinae and Calpinae within the family Erebidae, the latter holds this title now. Currently, Noctuidae is the second largest family in Noctuoidea, with about 1,089 genera and 11,772 species. This classification is still unsettled, as more changes continue to appear between Noctuidae and Erebidae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Noctua_pronuba%2C_Large_Yellow_Underwing%2C_Minera%2C_North_Wales%2C_July_2017_%2835698935536%29.jpg/330px-Noctua_pronuba%2C_Large_Yellow_Underwing%2C_Minera%2C_North_Wales%2C_July_2017_%2835698935536%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noctuidae","wikidata":"Q459180"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Eastern_Screech_Owl.jpg/500px-Eastern_Screech_Owl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Eastern_Screech_Owl.jpg/1280px-Eastern_Screech_Owl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Eastern_Screech_Owl.jpg/500px-Eastern_Screech_Owl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEastern_Screetch-Owl.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Strix_nebulosa_plumage.jpg/500px-Strix_nebulosa_plumage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Strix_nebulosa_plumage.jpg/1280px-Strix_nebulosa_plumage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Strix_nebulosa_plumage.jpg/500px-Strix_nebulosa_plumage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cross sectioned great grey owl specimen showing the extent of the body plumage, Zoological Museum, Copenhagen","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStrix_nebulosa_plumage.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Squelette_de_Strigidae_MHNT.jpg/500px-Squelette_de_Strigidae_MHNT.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Squelette_de_Strigidae_MHNT.jpg/1280px-Squelette_de_Strigidae_MHNT.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb